## ***********************************************************************
## **
## **  KB.INC.BL -- the keyboard buffer, emptied.
## **
## **      KB.CLEARKB     throw away whatever is already typed
## **
## **  ONE ROUTINE, ON ITS OWN, because the cheapest module in the tree should be
## **  cheap to reach. A drain belongs to programs that draw and to modules that
## **  read keys, and neither should have to include the screen etiquette or the
## **  dialog library to get four lines.
## **
## **  NOT A KEYWORD: a GET loop, no bulk data, so by the rule the GP set is built
## **  on it belongs in BASIC.
## **
## **  REQUIRES, and does not #INCLUDE for you:
## **      GPB.INC.BL        GP.DO, GP.EXITDO, GP.ISEMPTY
## **
## **  The whole KB.* name space belongs to this file.
## **
## ***********************************************************************
#IFNDEF KB.DEFS
#DEFINE KB.DEFS 1

GOTO KB.MODULE.END

## KB.CLEARKB -- throw away whatever is already typed.
##
## Call it before opening a dialog, and after a keystroke that redraws. Two things go
## wrong without it. A MODAL DIALOG opened with a key still in the buffer reads that key
## the instant it starts, answers itself and closes -- which reads as the dialog never
## having appeared. And a HELD ARROW repeats faster than a page can be drawn, so the
## queue keeps scrolling after the key has been let go.
##
## GET hands back "" on an empty buffer, so the drain stops at the first empty read.

KB.CLEARKB:
    GP.DO
        GET KB.K$
        IF GP.ISEMPTY(KB.K$) THEN GP.EXITDO
    GP.LOOP
    RETURN

KB.MODULE.END:
#ENDIF
